2026-10-28T17:30:43.7252042Z {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016eM8ZhBj4rFQQkFVSfb135","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"number\": 19,\n3\t  \"url\": \"https://github.com/wzinl/cs467-as3-triage-agent/issues/19\",\n4\t  \"repo\": \"wzinl/cs467-as3-triage-agent\",\n5\t  \"title\": \"aws_eks_cluster reports recurring drift when AWS automatically updates platform_version\",\n6\t  \"body\": \"### Terraform and AWS Provider Version\\n\\n```console\\nTerraform version: Any / Latest\\nAWS provider version: Any / Latest\\n```\\n\\n### Affected Resource(s) or Data Source(s)\\n\\n- `aws_eks_cluster`\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nAWS-managed changes to platform_version should not be reported as actionable\\nconfiguration drift, because:\\nThe attribute is read-only/computed.\\nUsers cannot select or pin an EKS platform version.\\nAWS upgrades it automatically.\\nThe Kubernetes minor version remains pinned and unchanged.\\nTerraform cannot revert the platform version.\\nIdeally, the provider should treat this attribute in a way that prevents\\nAWS-managed platform updates from producing recurring drift notifications,\\nwhile continuing to expose the current platform version as a readable\\nattribute.\\nIf provider-level suppression is not technically possible, guidance on the\\nrecommended approach for drift-detection platforms would be appreciated.\\n\\n### Actual Behavior\\n\\nThe provider refresh reports the AWS-managed platform version update as\\nout-of-band resource drift:\\nplatform_version = \\\"eks.23\\\" -> \\\"eks.24\\\"\\nThe user cannot configure or pin platform_version through the EKS API.\\nWhen aws_eks_cluster is created by a child module, users also cannot attach a\\nresource-level lifecycle.ignore_changes rule from the parent module.\\nA refresh-only state synchronization temporarily clears the drift, but it\\nreturns whenever AWS performs another automatic platform update.\\n\\n### Relevant Error/Panic Output\\n\\n```console\\n\\n```\\n\\n\\n### Sample Terraform Configuration\\n\\nterraform {\\n  required_providers {\\n    aws = {\\n      source  = \\\"hashicorp/aws\\\"\\n      version = \\\"<insert version>\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nprovider \\\"aws\\\" {\\n  region = \\\"eu-central-1\\\"\\n}\\n\\nresource \\\"aws_eks_cluster\\\" \\\"this\\\" {\\n  name     = \\\"example\\\"\\n  version  = \\\"1.34\\\"\\n  role_arn = aws_iam_role.cluster.arn\\n\\n  vpc_config {\\n    subnet_ids = var.subnet_ids\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n\\n\\n### Steps to Reproduce\\n\\n- Create an EKS cluster with a pinned Kubernetes minor version.\\n- Run terraform apply or tofu apply.\\n- Wait for AWS to automatically advance the EKS platform version.\\n- Run terraform plan, tofu plan, or a refresh-only drift check.\\n- Observe platform_version reported as a change made outside Terraform.\\n- The same behavior can be reproduced by importing or refreshing a cluster whose\\n- platform version has changed since the state was last written.\\n\\n### Debug Logging\\n\\nThis is particularly problematic in compliance environments where any\\nout-of-band change reported by Terraform is treated as a failed drift\\ncheck.\\nUsing -refresh=false is not an acceptable workaround because it would also\\nhide genuine infrastructure drift. Repeated refresh-only state updates reduce\\nthe reporting window but do not resolve the recurring behavior.\\n\\n\\n### GenAI / LLM Assisted Development\\n\\nn/a\\n\\n### Important Facts and References\\n\\nAWS EKS automatically upgrades the platform version of an existing cluster\\nwithout changing its Kubernetes minor version.\\nThe AWS provider exposes platform_version as a computed attribute on\\naws_eks_cluster. When AWS performs an automatic platform upgrade, every\\nsubsequent refresh detects the remote value changing from the value stored in\\nstate.\\nFor example:\\nNote: Objects have changed outside of Terraform\\n\\n# aws_eks_cluster.this has changed\\n~ resource \\\"aws_eks_cluster\\\" \\\"this\\\" {\\n    name             = \\\"apps-dev-sandbox\\\"\\n  ~ platform_version = \\\"eks.23\\\" -> \\\"eks.24\\\"\\n}\\nThe configured Kubernetes version remains unchanged:\\nversion = \\\"1.34\\\"\\nNo user, Terraform run, or external infrastructure automation changed\\nthe cluster. The change was performed automatically by the EKS service.\\nThis causes recurring false-positive drift reports in automated drift-detection\\n\\n\\n### Would you like to implement a fix?\\n\\nNo\\n\",\n7\t  \"author\": \"zzzinn\",\n8\t  \"author_association\": \"NONE\",\n9\t  \"labels\": [\n10\t    \"bug\"\n11\t  ],\n12\t  \"created_at\": \"2026-10-28T17:08:45Z\",\n13\t  \"as_of\": \"2026-06-22T13:41:37Z\"\n14\t}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4e5c1309-7517-49c3-9b99-3961bbf16623","uuid":"d8ac0bc2-d55c-4f59-8996-13aa714704d9","timestamp":"2026-10-28T17:30:43.718Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/gh-aw/agent/issue.json","content":"{\n  \"number\": 19,\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/wzinl/cs467-as3-triage-agent/issues/19\",\n  \"repo\": \"wzinl/cs467-as3-triage-agent\",\n  \"title\": \"aws_eks_cluster reports recurring drift when AWS automatically updates platform_version\",\n  \"body\": \"### Terraform and AWS Provider Version\\n\\n```console\\nTerraform version: Any / Latest\\nAWS provider version: Any / Latest\\n```\\n\\n### Affected Resource(s) or Data Source(s)\\n\\n- `aws_eks_cluster`\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nAWS-managed changes to platform_version should not be reported as actionable\\nconfiguration drift, because:\\nThe attribute is read-only/computed.\\nUsers cannot select or pin an EKS platform version.\\nAWS upgrades it automatically.\\nThe Kubernetes minor version remains pinned and unchanged.\\nTerraform cannot revert the platform version.\\nIdeally, the provider should treat this attribute in a way that prevents\\nAWS-managed platform updates from producing recurring drift notifications,\\nwhile continuing to expose the current platform version as a readable\\nattribute.\\nIf provider-level suppression is not technically possible, guidance on the\\nrecommended approach for drift-detection platforms would be appreciated.\\n\\n### Actual Behavior\\n\\nThe provider refresh reports the AWS-managed platform version update as\\nout-of-band resource drift:\\nplatform_version = \\\"eks.23\\\" -> \\\"eks.24\\\"\\nThe user cannot configure or pin platform_version through the EKS API.\\nWhen aws_eks_cluster is created by a child module, users also cannot attach a\\nresource-level lifecycle.ignore_changes rule from the parent module.\\nA refresh-only state synchronization temporarily clears the drift, but it\\nreturns whenever AWS performs another automatic platform update.\\n\\n### Relevant Error/Panic Output\\n\\n```console\\n\\n```\\n\\n\\n### Sample Terraform Configuration\\n\\nterraform {\\n  required_providers {\\n    aws = {\\n      source  = \\\"hashicorp/aws\\\"\\n      version = \\\"<insert version>\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nprovider \\\"aws\\\" {\\n  region = \\\"eu-central-1\\\"\\n}\\n\\nresource \\\"aws_eks_cluster\\\" \\\"this\\\" {\\n  name     = \\\"example\\\"\\n  version  = \\\"1.34\\\"\\n  role_arn = aws_iam_role.cluster.arn\\n\\n  vpc_config {\\n    subnet_ids = var.subnet_ids\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n\\n\\n### Steps to Reproduce\\n\\n- Create an EKS cluster with a pinned Kubernetes minor version.\\n- Run terraform apply or tofu apply.\\n- Wait for AWS to automatically advance the EKS platform version.\\n- Run terraform plan, tofu plan, or a refresh-only drift check.\\n- Observe platform_version reported as a change made outside Terraform.\\n- The same behavior can be reproduced by importing or refreshing a cluster whose\\n- platform version has changed since the state was last written.\\n\\n### Debug Logging\\n\\nThis is particularly problematic in compliance environments where any\\nout-of-band change reported by Terraform is treated as a failed drift\\ncheck.\\nUsing -refresh=false is not an acceptable workaround because it would also\\nhide genuine infrastructure drift. Repeated refresh-only state updates reduce\\nthe reporting window but do not resolve the recurring behavior.\\n\\n\\n### GenAI / LLM Assisted Development\\n\\nn/a\\n\\n### Important Facts and References\\n\\nAWS EKS automatically upgrades the platform version of an existing cluster\\nwithout changing its Kubernetes minor version.\\nThe AWS provider exposes platform_version as a computed attribute on\\naws_eks_cluster. When AWS performs an automatic platform upgrade, every\\nsubsequent refresh detects the remote value changing from the value stored in\\nstate.\\nFor example:\\nNote: Objects have changed outside of Terraform\\n\\n# aws_eks_cluster.this has changed\\n~ resource \\\"aws_eks_cluster\\\" \\\"this\\\" {\\n    name             = \\\"apps-dev-sandbox\\\"\\n  ~ platform_version = \\\"eks.23\\\" -> \\\"eks.24\\\"\\n}\\nThe configured Kubernetes version remains unchanged:\\nversion = \\\"1.34\\\"\\nNo user, Terraform run, or external infrastructure automation changed\\nthe cluster. The change was performed automatically by the EKS service.\\nThis causes recurring false-positive drift reports in automated drift-detection\\n\\n\\n### Would you like to implement a fix?\\n\\nNo\\n\",\n  \"author\": \"zzzinn\",\n  \"author_association\": \"NONE\",\n  \"labels\": [\n    \"bug\"\n  ],\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-10-28T17:08:45Z\",\n  \"as_of\": \"2026-06-22T13:41:37Z\"\n}","numLines":14,"startLine":1,"totalLines":14}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016eM8ZhBj4rFQQkFVSfb135","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
